# Genus OS instance environment — copy to /etc/robothor/robothor.env
# All systemd services source this file.

# Workspace root — where brain/, docs/agents/, memory/ live
ROBOTHOR_WORKSPACE=/home/robothor/robothor

# System user the services run as — scripts/install-units.sh renders the
# unit templates' User=robothor/Group=robothor placeholder lines to this.
ROBOTHOR_SERVICE_USER=robothor

# The service account's group. Optional: it defaults to ROBOTHOR_SERVICE_USER,
# which is the shape of every instance that has not deliberately split the two.
# Set it only if they differ. It fills Group= in the units and the `su` directive
# in /etc/logrotate.d/robothor, without which logrotate skips every workspace log
# whose directory is group-writable.
#ROBOTHOR_SERVICE_GROUP=robothor
HOME=/home/robothor
PATH=/home/robothor/.local/bin:/usr/local/bin:/usr/bin:/bin

# Database
ROBOTHOR_DB_USER=robothor
ROBOTHOR_DB_NAME=robothor_memory
ROBOTHOR_DB_HOST=/var/run/postgresql

# Instance identity
ROBOTHOR_OWNER_NAME=Owner
ROBOTHOR_OWNER_EMAIL=owner@example.com
ROBOTHOR_AI_NAME=Genus
ROBOTHOR_AI_EMAIL=ai@example.com
ROBOTHOR_AI_PHONE=              # e.g. "+1 (555) 123-4567" — quote if value contains spaces or parens
ROBOTHOR_AI_DOMAIN=
ROBOTHOR_TELEGRAM_BOT_NAME=

# Liveness watchdog (robothor-liveness.timer → scripts/liveness_probe.sh).
# Defaults shown; uncomment to change. See docs/runbooks/PAGING.md.
#ROBOTHOR_LIVENESS_URL=http://127.0.0.1:18800/live
#ROBOTHOR_LIVENESS_FAILURE_THRESHOLD=3
#ROBOTHOR_LIVENESS_TIMEOUT=10
#ROBOTHOR_LIVENESS_UNIT=robothor-engine.service

# Pager spool (scripts/send_failure_alert.sh). A page that could not be
# delivered — DNS down at boot — is written here and re-sent by the next send
# or the next liveness tick, which runs `send_failure_alert.sh --drain`.
# /var/lib, not /run: the boot window that eats a page usually ends in a reboot.
#ROBOTHOR_ALERT_SPOOL_DIR=/var/lib/robothor/alert-spool
#ROBOTHOR_ALERT_SPOOL_CAP=50
# A page the drain cannot deliver is moved to <spool>/poison/ rather than
# left at the head of the queue, where it used to block every page behind
# it: after this many failed attempts (~4h of 5-minute ticks), or once it
# is this old. Anything in poison/ was NEVER delivered.
#ROBOTHOR_ALERT_SPOOL_MAX_ATTEMPTS=48
#ROBOTHOR_ALERT_SPOOL_MAX_AGE_SECONDS=86400

# Offsite backup (scripts/backup-offsite.sh, robothor-backup-offsite.timer).
# ROBOTHOR_OFFSITE_KEEP is your recovery window — SET IT EXPLICITLY. On the
# first instance it was set nowhere and rode the script default, then silently
# moved 5 -> 7 when a unit rewrite dropped whatever had been exporting it.
# Retention depth must be a decision, not an accident.
#ROBOTHOR_OFFSITE_REMOTE=r2:robothor-backups
#ROBOTHOR_OFFSITE_KEEP=7

# ── Nightly WildClawBench rotation (robothor-bench-rotation.timer) ──
# Instance-provisioned: a WildClawBench checkout, its downloaded workspace
# fixtures, and a durable output root for the ledger. See bench/wildclaw/.
WILDCLAW_REPO=/opt/robothor-bench/WildClawBench
WILDCLAW_DATA=/opt/robothor-bench/wcb-data
WILDCLAW_OUT=/opt/robothor-bench/out

# Persist each assistant turn onto its agent_run_steps row so a finished
# run's reasoning chain can be read back (diagnosis, replay). Off by default:
# a turn carries whatever the agent was reasoning about. Capped per turn.
#ROBOTHOR_RECORD_ASSISTANT_TURNS=true

# ── Execution mode: local and cloud are peer modes, not exception and normal ──
# The engine derives which economics apply from what actually SERVED a request,
# never from an outage flag and never from the credential pool (KeyPool
# .exhausted() mutates the state it reports and flips optimistically healthy
# every 6h against a weekly cap). Pin it only if you mean to.
#   auto  = observed (default)
#   local = a deliberately local-only instance. This is a supported
#           configuration, not a permanent alarm.
#   cloud = never repolicy, whatever is serving.
#ROBOTHOR_EXECUTION_MODE=auto

# How many local model requests may be in flight at once. Discovered, in order:
# this variable, then OLLAMA_NUM_PARALLEL in the engine's environment, then the
# ollama unit's own Environment=, then a conservative default of 1. Set it only
# to override a probe that is wrong — a wrong value here throttles or overloads
# the device. `robothor plugin` and /health both report which source won.
#ROBOTHOR_LOCAL_MAX_CONCURRENT=

# ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
# GUARDRAIL POSTURE DOES NOT BELONG IN THIS FILE. Leave these commented.
# ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
# systemd applies this file via EnvironmentFile= AFTER the drop-in's
# Environment= directives, so a guardrail set here wins outright — and this
# file is instance data, so it is not in git and no test in CI can see it.
# The mirror test (tests/test_flag_manifest.py) reads only
# infra/systemd/robothor-engine.service.d/upgrade-rip-flags.conf.
#
# On 2026-09-02 three controls were found living only here:
# completion-contracts (enforce), admission (enforce) and the deliverable
# contract (observe). infra/flags.yaml said admission was in `observe`; it had
# been enforcing since 2026-08-27, and nothing in git recorded it. A rebuilt
# instance would have come up without all three, silently.
#
# So: posture goes in upgrade-rip-flags.conf AND infra/flags.yaml, in the same
# PR. See docs/runbooks/GUARDRAIL_FLIPS.md. The lines below are documentation
# of what exists, not configuration to uncomment — uncommenting one takes the
# flag back out of version control. `scripts/flag_audit.py` prints which layer
# is actually winning for every flag.
#
# Fleet admission control (the FleetPool gate). Ladder: off -> observe ->
# alert -> enforce. `observe` records the deferral it WOULD have made and runs
# the agent anyway, so promotion rests on agent_guardrail_events rows rather
# than on faith. Governed — flippable at runtime from the Controls dashboard,
# no restart.
#ROBOTHOR_ADMISSION_ENABLED=1
#ROBOTHOR_ADMISSION_MODE=observe

# Deliverable contract: did the run produce the artifact the TASK named? The
# complement of completion-contracts, which asks whether the agent's CLAIMS
# are backed by its trace. An agent passes that and fails this by doing the
# work correctly and saving it to the wrong path — measured 2026-08-26 as
# -0.87 of a -1.04 competitive gap. Ladder: off -> observe -> alert ->
# enforce. See docs/runbooks/DELIVERABLE_CONTRACT.md.
#ROBOTHOR_DELIVERABLE_CONTRACT_ENABLED=1
#ROBOTHOR_DELIVERABLE_CONTRACT_MODE=observe

# Make sandbox `enforce` actually govern the agents that opted out. A manifest
# `sandbox: host` is honoured BEFORE the mode is consulted, so four of the six
# exec-holding agents are unaffected by the global mode. Under `observe` this
# changes no behaviour but makes them APPEAR in the would-block set, so you can
# see what enforce would newly capture before flipping it. Under `enforce` it
# containerises them. Read GUARDRAIL_FLIPS.md before setting it.
#ROBOTHOR_SANDBOX_ENFORCE_OVERRIDES_MANIFEST=1

# Plugin manifest admission: refuse a distribution that ships no
# genus-plugin.yaml, BEFORE ep.load() imports its code into the daemon. Every
# other check in the loader runs after the import. Ladder: observe -> enforce;
# observe does NOT protect, it only reports. Meaningless on an instance with no
# genus.* entry points installed.
#ROBOTHOR_PLUGIN_MANIFEST_ENABLED=1
#ROBOTHOR_PLUGIN_MANIFEST_MODE=observe

# The role a federated or service principal gets when none is assigned. A
# fresh instance seeds no `service` role at all, which denies every agent every
# tool; this is the knob that says what the default should be. Widening it
# widens what every unassigned principal may do — change it deliberately.
#ROBOTHOR_DEFAULT_SERVICE_ROLE=service

# ⚠ DANGEROUS — the global kill switch. Set to 1 and EVERY guardrail above
# stops enforcing at once: RBAC, injection scan, exec allowlist, approvals,
# sandbox, admission, the contracts. It exists for a diagnostic hour with an
# operator watching. A fleet left running with this set is a fleet running with
# no controls and a dashboard that still reads `enforce`. flag_audit.py reports
# it as DEBUG-ENV whenever it is present. Unset it when you are done.
#ROBOTHOR_DISABLE_ALL_GUARDRAILS=0

# The offline tier. Terminates EVERY agent's model chain (config._with_last_resort),
# so it is what stall budgets are validated against — a manifest whose stall
# budget is below this model's per-call allowance is guaranteed to kill healthy
# runs, not merely likely to. config_schema warns when that happens.
#ROBOTHOR_LAST_RESORT_MODEL=ollama_chat/qwen3.8:27b

# Thermal thresholds, in Celsius. Shared with scripts/thermal-guard.sh — ONE
# policy, two consumers (the shell guard caps CPU frequency; the engine sheds
# concurrency). A test parses the script and fails if the two ever drift, so
# change them here and there together or not at all.
#ROBOTHOR_THERMAL_THROTTLE_C=85
#ROBOTHOR_THERMAL_WARN_C=90
#ROBOTHOR_THERMAL_CRIT_C=94
#ROBOTHOR_THERMAL_RESTORE_C=75

# ── Local inference gate: pacing, not just parallelism ──
# Measured on GB10 (docs/runbooks/THERMAL.md): package temperature tracks GPU power
# at ~0.65 C/W, one 27B stream draws ~62W and plateaus near 85C, and a single
# 7.5k-token request lifts the package 22C in 11s. Two back-to-back requests cross
# THROTTLE_C. So there is no concurrency setting at which continuous local work is
# safe — the gate also declines to START new work while hot, and resumes once cool.
# Recovery is near-instantaneous, which is what makes this cheap rather than costly.
#   PACE_BACKGROUND_C : cron, sweeps and reranking yield here (below THROTTLE_C)
#   PACE_ALL_C        : everything except an operator's turn yields here
#   RESUME_C          : latched — paced work resumes only after cooling to this
# An operator's turn is never paced, and an unreadable sensor never paces at all.
#ROBOTHOR_LOCAL_PACE_BACKGROUND_C=80
#ROBOTHOR_LOCAL_PACE_ALL_C=85
#ROBOTHOR_LOCAL_PACE_RESUME_C=75
#ROBOTHOR_LOCAL_GATE_WAIT_SECONDS=120

# Resume runs that were in flight when the engine restarted. OFF by default:
# this changes what a restart MEANS — an interrupted run is re-driven rather
# than left terminal — so it is a decision, not a default. Verify it recovered
# something by counting rows, never from a log line the resume path prints
# about itself.
#ROBOTHOR_RESUME_IN_FLIGHT=1

# How long a credential sits out after a PERIODIC (weekly/monthly) quota cap,
# in seconds. Default 21600 (6h). The failure this exists for: a weekly cap
# retried every 15 minutes is 96 pointless revivals a day, each one an error
# the operator sees. Too long and a recovered key stays benched; too short and
# the fleet hammers a capped credential.
#ROBOTHOR_PERIODIC_QUOTA_COOLDOWN_SECONDS=21600

# Slots held back from BACKGROUND work so an operator's turn is never third in line
# behind cron. Read by daemon.py when it builds the FleetPool. A malformed value
# raises at daemon boot rather than degrading, so set it to a plain integer or
# leave it unset.
#ROBOTHOR_RESERVED_INTERACTIVE_SLOTS=1

# ── GPU clock cap (robothor-gpu-clock-cap.service) ──
# The only hardware bound on GB10: the part exposes no power limit, so clock is
# what bounds watts, and watts are what package temperature tracks. Measured
# 2026-08-28: uncapped the 27B plateaus at 86C/69W; at 1500 MHz it plateaus at
# 76C/28W for ~10% less generation. Uncapped is SLOWER than 2000 MHz and 11C
# hotter. Do not raise above 2000 without new measurements.
#ROBOTHOR_GPU_CLOCK_CAP_MHZ=1500
#ROBOTHOR_GPU_CLOCK_MIN_MHZ=300

# ── Thermal shedding (robothor-thermal-shed.service) ──
# The half that stops workload, so the peer guard's 94C clean reboot is never
# reached. The package budget is shared by CPU and GPU: one 27B stream alone
# plateaus ~85C, but that stream plus a saturated CPU reached 96C on 2026-08-28,
# so the rungs must leave room for the CPU term (docs/runbooks/THERMAL.md).
# POLL matters as much as the thresholds — at ~2C/s a 10s poll can miss 20C, and
# on 2026-08-28 this guard read 85C then 96C and fired one second too late.
#ROBOTHOR_SHED_STAGE1_C=82
#ROBOTHOR_SHED_STAGE2_C=86
#ROBOTHOR_SHED_RECOVER_C=78
#ROBOTHOR_SHED_STAGE1_SUSTAIN=6
#ROBOTHOR_SHED_POLL=2

# ── Crash-loop breaker (robothor-boot-guard.service) ──
# This instance is administered remotely. A reboot loop costs administrative
# access, so after LIMIT boots inside WINDOW seconds the guard inhibits inference
# and leaves the box up, idle and reachable rather than cycling. Clear with
#   sudo rm -f /run/robothor/INHIBIT_INFERENCE /var/lib/robothor/boot-history
#ROBOTHOR_BOOT_LOOP_WINDOW=900
#ROBOTHOR_BOOT_LOOP_LIMIT=3

# Fleet-wide hourly spend cap. Applies in CLOUD mode only: local inference has
# no marginal cost, and both local models are registered at 0.0/token, so a
# monetary governor there would be theatre. Local scarcity is heat, resident
# memory and inference slots — see the thermal knobs above.
#ROBOTHOR_HOURLY_COST_CAP_USD=5.0

# Absolute wall-clock backstop per run, before tier scaling. The local tier
# multiplies this by the chain's tempo factor, CAPPED at 2x — a backstop that
# scales without limit is a suggestion.
#ROBOTHOR_MAX_WALLCLOCK_SECONDS=3600

# ── Federation (NATS broker) ──
# Only needed on an instance that federates. The engine cannot connect to its
# own broker without the credential, and `federation invite` cannot write a
# peer account without the broker config path. See docs/runbooks/FEDERATION.md.
#ROBOTHOR_NATS_URL=nats://127.0.0.1:4222
#ROBOTHOR_NATS_USER=engine
#ROBOTHOR_NATS_PASSWORD=            # from the broker config; keep it out of git
#ROBOTHOR_NATS_CONFIG=/etc/nats/nats-server.conf

# ⚠ Federate with tenancy NOT enforced. Every inbound op runs inside
# tenant_scope, but that layer is only real while row-level security is
# enforcing — so activation REFUSES when RLS is inert. This override accepts
# that inbound ops are not tenant-isolated. Reasonable for two of your own
# instances on one box; not for a peer you do not control. The fix, not the
# override, is ROBOTHOR_RLS_ENABLED=1 with a non-superuser DB user
# (docs/runbooks/TENANT_RLS.md).
#ROBOTHOR_FEDERATION_ALLOW_INERT_RLS=1
